WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.953
- Hi guys,

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:02.902
my name is Heinrich from Firm Learning.

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:03.735
Welcome back,

00:00:03.735 --> 00:00:04.940
and thank you for watching.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.520
This video is another video in my series,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.254
on how to improve your management
business presentations,

00:00:12.254 --> 00:00:14.690
to adopt a style that is followed

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.890
by top management consulting firms

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.090
like BCG, Bain or McKinsey.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.820
This video focuses on slide blueprints.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.960
Very frequently you are in a situation

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.521
where in a short amount of time,

00:00:25.521 --> 00:00:28.299
you need to produce a significant number

00:00:28.299 --> 00:00:31.520
of PowerPoint pages of
slides for a presentation.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.784
In these situations, what is just crucial

00:00:33.784 --> 00:00:36.030
is that you do not need to start

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.800
for every single page to ask yourself

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.400
well, how do I put the
information on paper?

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.527
What structure do I use?

00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:46.820
How exact do I present it?

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.410
If you do that you will
take hours and hours

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.630
to even create basic
PowerPoint presentations.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.650
What's crucial and what
people in consulting do

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.140
is they use slide blueprints.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.850
There are certain structures
that they can adopt,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.000
and just reuse,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.700
and where they know is that if I present

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.860
the information this specific way,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.770
then it will be at least good enough,

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.282
everybody will clearly understand

00:01:08.282 --> 00:01:09.850
what I'm going to say,

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.000
maybe it's not the most
fancy, most beautiful page,

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.820
but it's going to meet
professional standards

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.970
and I can bring my information across.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.360
And as you maybe know,

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.470
frequently in consulting,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.290
you're in a situation
where, literally overnight,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.722
you need to create presentations
of 10, 20, 30 pages,

00:01:25.722 --> 00:01:28.840
and here it's just crucial
that you are in a position

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.990
to be able to produce this content quickly

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:33.780
and to a high level of quality

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.320
and without slide blueprints in this way,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.930
this is just impossible.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.021
As the other videos on creating
PowerPoint presentations,

00:01:41.021 --> 00:01:44.380
This video is an excerpt on my course.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.057
You can find a link to
the video description,

00:01:47.057 --> 00:01:49.075
if you would like to check it out.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.940
And please have a look at the reviews

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.120
of the other students who already took it,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.170
if you ask yourself,

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.230
is this actually the right thing for you.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:57.522
But now without further ado,

00:01:57.522 --> 00:01:59.070
let's dive into it.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.020
Let's learn about slide blueprints,

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.290
I trust that this is
really going to be helpful

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.490
in improving your production
flow in the future.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:07.700
So before we jump

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.260
into some very specific slide blueprints,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.940
let me just talk about
some general principles.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.670
And first I'd like to talk
about things to avoid,

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.230
because then you know
that these are things

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:20.220
that you would definitely
not use on your pages.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.530
And then also some key
principles to follow,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.370
these are things that
you should look after,

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:28.080
because they will help your
slide production process.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.670
So first some things to avoid.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.913
So, and maybe also just to start,

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.794
I mean, these are just
kind of rule of thumbs.

00:02:34.794 --> 00:02:37.320
So it might be that in some cases,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.880
it does make sense and it
is okay to do these things.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.970
Just from my experience
that most in of the cases,

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.517
you do not want to do this, okay.

00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:49.270
So the first point to avoid
are extensive color fields,

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.620
especially behind text.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.370
And a color field would
be something like this,

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:57.810
so kind of a box that
is filled with a color,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.130
and what some people like to do

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.030
is they like to take these kind of boxes,

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.870
and put them below the text.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.090
So imagine this kind of list of bullets

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:11.000
would have such a blue box behind them.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.687
And some people do that a lot,

00:03:12.687 --> 00:03:16.090
and they feel like this
somehow looks good or whatever.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:17.533
And I mean, I don't wanna argue with you,

00:03:17.533 --> 00:03:19.150
whether it looks good or not

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.530
because I guess this is subjective.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.695
But I think there's at
least two strong reasons

00:03:24.695 --> 00:03:26.490
why you shouldn't do that.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:27.880
So first,

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.000
if kind of you have it on a beamer right,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:30.833
the presentation,

00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:32.770
or maybe the beamer isn't
so good or whatever.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.270
So the kind of project
off the presentation,

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:38.620
it just might not be a really
good read of it, right.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:42.190
So if you have kind of a color
below or behind your text,

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:43.570
the contrast isn't really good.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.940
So just kind of makes it more difficult

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.320
for the reader to read.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.770
And the second reason is and
where this kind of becomes

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.039
even more relevant, if you print it out.

00:03:52.039 --> 00:03:54.120
And what you will see very often is that

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.190
these presentations are being printed out

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:57.350
in black and white.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.040
Many corporates have even policies,

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.690
to whenever possible,
only print out things

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:04.329
in black and white without color.

00:04:04.329 --> 00:04:06.960
because it saves cost of course.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.650
And even though maybe on your computer,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.993
if you have it with a color,

00:04:11.993 --> 00:04:13.749
in the textbox behind it,

00:04:13.749 --> 00:04:16.310
maybe then it looks good,
it looks reasonable.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.080
It looks very readable.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.690
But it's often very hard to kind of know

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.692
how it will look like if you print it out

00:04:22.692 --> 00:04:25.700
on a black and white kind of color scheme,

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.300
especially maybe if the
printer isn't really good.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.541
And then you would easily
find yourself in a situation

00:04:30.541 --> 00:04:32.810
where the text on the bullets

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:35.310
is not really very
readable anymore, right?

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.080
So avoid extensive color fields.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.810
This is my kind of recommendation

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:40.260
from my experience having done this

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.790
for many, many years, avoid them,

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:45.350
they add no value to your presentation.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.610
And there are lots of risks
in terms of readability

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:48.847
if you do that.

00:04:48.847 --> 00:04:50.670
And let me just kind of to contrast it

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.693
to what I did here, of
course you could argue,

00:04:52.693 --> 00:04:54.470
well then you know what did you do

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:55.480
here on the left, right,

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:56.987
because this is also a color field.

00:04:56.987 --> 00:05:00.340
And while I do like to
use color fields I get

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.480
for kind of titles, right?

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.270
Or if you would like
to highlight something,

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.070
and you could say this is kind of a title.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.320
And this is why this is highlighted.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.241
And this is kind of the
general texts field.

00:05:12.241 --> 00:05:15.970
And so would say this is kind
of a bit of a gray area thing,

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.010
because it's usually only
little text that you have here

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.250
or just kind of really adds meaning,

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:22.660
because this highlight

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.710
really means that kind
of on a hierarchy level,

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.007
this stands above the other text field,

00:05:27.007 --> 00:05:29.270
I think you can justify it this way,

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.419
then probably you could easily also have,

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:33.120
done this without this text field,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.490
just having bolded this or whatever.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.160
So just think about if
it's really required

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.910
in any given instance that you wanna do,

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:42.340
and if in doubt, leave it out.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:44.623
Don't do it and you
will usually be better.

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:49.677
Another general example that
I can just really urge you

00:05:49.677 --> 00:05:54.110
not to do is the usage of
shadows and 3D elements,

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:56.340
and probably especially 3D elements.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.662
I mean, some light shadows
can sometimes be okay.

00:05:59.662 --> 00:06:01.180
But this is also the idea

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:05.315
that some people just think
that the presentation,

00:06:05.315 --> 00:06:06.507
to make it good,

00:06:06.507 --> 00:06:09.070
you just need to make it look fancy.

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:11.000
And what does it mean
to make it look fancy?

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.380
Well, you want to add as
much effects and so on,

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.290
that are somehow available in PowerPoint

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.353
to all the different objects as possible.

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:21.356
And the problem is that, usually,

00:06:21.356 --> 00:06:23.470
this doesn't really make it look fancy,

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:25.480
it just looks stupid, right?

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:26.780
I mean, most of the cases,

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:28.760
it looks better if you don't apply it

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:30.230
than if you do apply it.

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.600
And indeed, in a professional context,

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.480
it just kind of considered bad style,

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.580
just kind of consider
probably even unprofessional

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.480
to create a presentation
which is full of shadows

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.157
and full of 3D elements.

00:06:42.157 --> 00:06:44.850
And probably the reason why
this is considered this way

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.060
is because it kind of gives the impression

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.770
that you were much more
concerned about adding

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.500
some kind of weird graphic
effects to a presentation

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.830
than about focusing on the content

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.322
and the things that really matter.

00:06:57.322 --> 00:06:59.530
And so I mean, in general,

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:00.480
you probably I mean,

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.120
you might sometimes see a shadow,

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.580
but you almost never will see one of these

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:07.910
kind of strange PowerPoint 3D elements,

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.690
or whatever your presentation program

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.510
is able to do on the page.

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:15.050
So just in general avoid it
and you will be fine off.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:17.660
And in a very similar direction goes

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:18.880
the thing of smart arts.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.212
So this is a PowerPoint
specific functionality,

00:07:21.212 --> 00:07:23.830
where PowerPoint allows you to create

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:28.430
some kind of frameworks,
some kind of process bars

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:31.750
and all kinds of different
variations of that on the page.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.760
And it's supposed to help
you to kind of create

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.920
these kind of frameworks
on your page more easily

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.880
than you would have to,
then know you would be able

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.890
to create them by yourself, if you just

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:43.870
kind of put it together yourself,

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.320
from all the individual
elements they consist of.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.020
But just from my experiences again,

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:50.600
is something that isn't really used

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.000
in a professional context.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.940
And the reason why is I mean,

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.637
if you use these kind of things,

00:07:55.637 --> 00:07:59.130
you will notice just that
it's just extremely difficult

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:03.120
to really make them look nice,
and clean and professional.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.050
And usually the font sizes
are all over the place,

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:08.028
they will be like
multiple font sizes in it,

00:08:08.028 --> 00:08:09.830
the proportions of the text

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:11.960
to the elements doesn't really look good.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:14.010
I mean, it just almost impossible

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.990
to kind of customize it and
make it look good, right?

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:19.930
And so basically what
everybody's doing is, I mean,

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.840
the visual itself are
of course okay, right,

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:26.490
it's totally fine to have
some kind of process bar logic

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:27.940
or whatever on your page.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.207
But usually what you would do

00:08:29.207 --> 00:08:30.330
is you would just create it yourself.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.370
You wouldn't use these pre-made templates.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:35.930
So basically you would go to
the icon section of PowerPoint,

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:39.370
and then you would just
kind of drag and drop

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:40.567
the individual elements that you need,

00:08:40.567 --> 00:08:42.970
and then it would just create
whatever you need yourself.

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:45.260
And then you have full flexibility

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:46.783
in editing it in a properly.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.100
Another point is unnecessary
background images.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.550
So some people just like to kind of put

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.270
some random images somewhere on the page

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:57.660
because they think it looks cool

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.330
and fancy and whatever.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:00.740
I mean, this is the same

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.000
and it goes back to all the
things that I just taught you.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.260
I mean, don't do it, right.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:05.680
Usually it doesn't add value.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:07.670
Of course, if you have an
image that does add value,

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:09.460
that is relevant to the presentation,

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:10.710
then of course add it,

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.316
but usually it just supposed
to be some kind of decoration,

00:09:13.316 --> 00:09:15.130
and then it's just distracting

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:16.737
from the things that you really wanna say,

00:09:16.737 --> 00:09:18.600
and that really are relevant.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:19.660
So don't do this, right,

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:21.480
it just doesn't make sense.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:24.790
Another point is multiple
font sizes on one page.

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:27.250
And what just as a general rule,

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:28.190
you want to avoid

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:32.570
is to having multiple font
sizes on the same page,

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:35.240
with the exception being
the action title, right?

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.790
So the action title, it's
fine if it's a bit larger,

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:40.000
but usually you never wanna have

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.440
different font sizes on one page,

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.920
because it really looks bad.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:47.640
I mean, the look and feel of
your slides is much better,

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:48.473
excuse me,

00:09:48.473 --> 00:09:50.340
the look and feel of your
slides is much better

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:52.420
if all the font sizes look the same.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:54.140
And if you look kind of the presentation

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:56.518
that I prepared here
throughout this course,

00:09:56.518 --> 00:09:58.187
I just hope that I never use

00:09:58.187 --> 00:10:00.980
multiple font sizes on one page.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.000
So just really try to avoid it.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.480
And most consulting firms is
actually a really hard rule.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.350
So you just don't do it.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:09.383
It's often tempting because sometimes,

00:10:09.383 --> 00:10:12.250
you just want to fit this in on the page,

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.250
and there just isn't the space,

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:18.260
and if you kind of just put this one box

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:19.480
in a smaller font size,

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:21.242
and everything would fit in nicely.

00:10:21.242 --> 00:10:23.130
And of course, it's a trade off,

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:24.990
and sometimes it saves you time.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:26.633
So if you wanna do this, then fine,

00:10:26.633 --> 00:10:28.790
I'm not going to stop you.

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:30.100
But in general,

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:31.460
if you just really want to make sure

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.180
that your presentation looks nice,

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:35.583
and clean and professional, don't do it.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:38.970
So what are the things that you should do?

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.470
What are the principles
that you should follow?

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.340
And here are two principles

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:45.307
that I would like to kind
of to quickly present to you

00:10:45.307 --> 00:10:47.740
and the first is every page has a table,

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:50.630
and every page speaks for itself.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:51.710
And on the first one,

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:54.020
I do not want to go into
too much detail right now,

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:57.520
because this is what we
were gonna focus on a detail

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:00.330
in the next I think two or so lectures.

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:04.030
And the concept, but just to
quickly introduce you to it

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:06.720
is that just in order
to make a good table,

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.052
just always think about sorry,

00:11:08.052 --> 00:11:09.950
in order to make a good page,

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:14.010
just always think about the
page as being a table, right.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.450
And for instance, on this example,

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.530
you could think of this page

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:19.860
as being a table with two columns.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:21.690
This is the first column.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.730
And this is the second column.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.990
So this is some kind of a title column,

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:27.800
where you kind of introduce

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.170
and name the things that
you wanna talk about.

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:31.547
And this is kind of a detail column

00:11:31.547 --> 00:11:33.870
where you give specifics and details

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.050
of the things that you're
talking about here.

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:37.330
oops sorry.

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:40.180
And so but let me just leave you at this

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:41.910
because we will go into detail

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:45.034
kind of on the next couple
of lectures on this.

00:11:45.034 --> 00:11:48.400
And so let me just spend some
words on the second principle,

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:50.630
every page speaks for itself.

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:52.250
And maybe you remember the section

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:54.170
or the lecture that we had on diagrams,

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:56.070
and I think there was one extra called,

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:57.828
every chart speaks for itself.

00:11:57.828 --> 00:12:00.550
And the concept here is the very same.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:02.603
And just to kind of give you an anecdote,

00:12:02.603 --> 00:12:05.370
when I was a young
consultant, I had a partner,

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:08.160
and he tried to explain
to me exactly this, right,

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.880
every page speaks for itself.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:13.560
And what he told me is kind
of the following instance,

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:14.570
he told me, you know,

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:18.563
Heinrich, imagine you are
working in a client room.

00:12:18.563 --> 00:12:20.660
And for whatever reason,

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:22.430
you were having a presentation there,

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:24.550
and then you left one of the pages

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:27.707
of the presentation in
the room, accidentally.

00:12:27.707 --> 00:12:30.150
Of course, this is something
that should never happen,

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.010
due to a whole lot of reasons.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:35.059
Probably the most important
one is confidentiality.

00:12:35.059 --> 00:12:38.130
But let's just imagine
that you left one page

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:39.577
in the client room.

00:12:39.577 --> 00:12:41.410
And now what you want to achieve,

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:42.520
what you want to aspire to,

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:45.340
whenever you create a presentation,

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:47.270
that basically a random person

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:48.967
could grab this one page that you left,

00:12:48.967 --> 00:12:52.600
could look at it, could
understand what it means

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.600
without anybody being there,

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.080
and would say, yeah okay,

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:57.900
this is solid piece of rock.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:12:59.900
This is a good page, right?

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:02.570
And so what it means
is probably two things.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:04.230
First, that you always make sure

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.450
that even though of course content

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:07.470
is the most important thing,

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.320
the things that you actually write on it,

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:12.220
you always make sure that
a certain minimum quality

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.900
of design is met, right.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:18.160
So I mean, your pages always
need to look well aligned,

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:21.070
need to look decent, and so on.

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:23.020
But then regarding the content,

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:25.490
that you make sure that
it is self-contained,

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:27.860
in the way that everybody can understand

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:31.250
the most important messages of the slide,

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:32.760
just by reading it themself,

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:36.020
without anybody needing
to explain it to him.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.760
So every page speaks for
itself is an important,

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:39.993
very important concept,

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:43.065
within kind of management presentations,

00:13:43.065 --> 00:13:46.140
and top management kind of areas.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:47.750
You know, when you talk with CEOs,

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:50.590
he wants to be able to
understand what you wanna say

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:52.780
himself without you needing to be there.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.258
So follow these two principles.

00:13:54.258 --> 00:13:56.320
And you slide creation process

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:57.813
will be much more straightforward

00:13:57.813 --> 00:14:00.050
and the next kind of topic now

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:02.040
that I really wanna
detail as I just told you

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.387
is every page has a table.

00:14:03.387 --> 00:14:06.640
And this is now the blueprint
aspect of this, right?

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:08.220
I mean, if you understand this,

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:10.080
you have a good blueprint
of all the pages.

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:13.020
So let's dive into this right now.

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:13.853
In this lecture,

00:14:13.853 --> 00:14:16.190
I'd like to build on
what we just discussed

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:17.972
in the last lecture,
namely, on the principle,

00:14:17.972 --> 00:14:20.686
every page as a table.

00:14:20.686 --> 00:14:24.060
And the reason I'm saying
this is the following,

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.030
what you need is a design blueprint,

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:28.420
that you can quickly
pull out of your pocket,

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:31.590
whenever you want to create
big presentation document

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:33.458
without needing to think every time

00:14:33.458 --> 00:14:35.580
kind of for lots of time,

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:36.860
how should the page look like,

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:40.210
how should the slide look
like, that I wanna create.

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:43.470
And this basic blueprint
means that you should always

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:46.910
think of every single page
that you create as a table.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:48.490
And what I would like to show you now

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:52.120
on the next kind of slides that
I would go through with you,

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:55.017
are a couple of examples
of how pages look like,

00:14:55.017 --> 00:14:57.650
and you would always see
this part on this in the end,

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.870
every page is just a table, right?

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:01.360
So you always have just ask yourself,

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:03.180
what is the information that I have?

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:05.270
And how can I arrange it in a way

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:08.310
that it fits nicely into a table.

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:10.670
So let's start with a simple text table.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:13.182
This is a very simple example
that we see right here.

00:15:13.182 --> 00:15:15.490
And I hope that you can recognize

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:17.250
this table form that you see here.

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:19.810
Basically, this thing has two columns,

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:21.640
measures and rationale.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:23.320
And then there are a couple of rows,

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:26.420
and you see every row and
this kind of example here

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:29.370
represents an area location here,

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:31.660
some kind of a customer segment and so on.

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:34.430
Let's not focused too much on the contents

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:36.350
of what's shown here,

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:38.410
but just rather than on the structure.

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:40.650
And this is something that
you will see quite frequently,

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:43.967
of some kind of a title column like that.

00:15:43.967 --> 00:15:46.130
And then you have some details provided

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:50.020
by the title or on the title
that was introduced here.

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:52.760
Of course, this page is quite wordy,

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:53.760
as you would say, right.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:55.060
So it has lots of text,

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:58.146
probably it's a bit too wordy
to put it in your main deck.

00:15:58.146 --> 00:15:59.740
This could be more something

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:01.406
like you would have in your backup.

00:16:01.406 --> 00:16:03.690
But this is kind of just a basic example

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:07.013
how you can arrange your
text in the table format.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.160
Now let's look at something similar,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.260
maybe a bit more advanced,
maybe a bit more complex.

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:14.670
And probably on first sight,

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:17.260
you would say, yes,
this is a good looking,

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.200
nice slide, right.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:20.033
But in the end,

00:16:20.033 --> 00:16:22.000
this just follows a table format as well.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:22.833
And as you can see,

00:16:22.833 --> 00:16:24.433
you have these different columns here.

00:16:24.433 --> 00:16:26.686
And then there is a bit
of space here on the right

00:16:26.686 --> 00:16:29.360
with this thing that kind of pops out,

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:31.540
you would call this a callout, right,

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:34.356
so you add details to specific elements.

00:16:34.356 --> 00:16:38.160
In this kind of context, it
is on the industry opportunity

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:39.586
expressing courier services,

00:16:39.586 --> 00:16:42.256
and on the industry
opportunity of coal mining.

00:16:42.256 --> 00:16:44.610
And you make this callout here

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:46.470
where you add this additional text.

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:49.070
But what you can see is
that in terms of design,

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:52.680
in the end, this is also
some kind of a table column,

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:54.160
just doesn't have a title,

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:57.930
but it fits nicely to the
right as a table column.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:16:59.990
And again, this is also typically designed

00:16:59.990 --> 00:17:02.940
where you have kind of
certain kinds of elements

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.060
that you wanna range,

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:06.360
you order them by one criterium.

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:09.810
And here it's a data point
that you use to all of them,

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:11.580
namely the revenue pool,

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:14.010
which is, a data point that we put here,

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:15.340
it could be anything else.

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:18.110
And then you provide
some additional details

00:17:18.110 --> 00:17:20.430
and their qualitative assessment.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:23.340
But again, this is now
rather complex information

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:25.370
that you have on these different elements,

00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:27.833
but it is presented in a table format.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:31.872
Just one more example.

00:17:31.872 --> 00:17:35.190
This again, is kind of
a page that summarizes

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:39.300
certain data points on
kind of different elements

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.650
in this case, we have
infrastructure data management,

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:44.330
analytics platforms and
services and support.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:48.240
So that these could be seen
as values, the process points

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.890
or maybe even industries
that you wanna look at.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:52.997
And again, here we have some data points,

00:17:52.997 --> 00:17:55.503
the CAGR component and your growth rate,

00:17:55.503 --> 00:17:58.406
and then a qualitative
assessment of some drivers,

00:17:58.406 --> 00:18:02.520
and this is very typical kind
of collection of information

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:03.660
that you would like to give

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:05.920
this top management style presentation.

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:08.063
And once again, you can transform it

00:18:08.063 --> 00:18:11.343
or kind of presented in the way
that it is shown as a table.

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:15.800
And the reason I'm putting so
much emphasis on this is that,

00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:18.020
in the end, when you create presentations,

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:19.657
and you need to create lots of pages

00:18:19.657 --> 00:18:21.640
in a short amount of time,

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:23.007
what it's important for you to have

00:18:23.007 --> 00:18:26.610
is kind of a basic blueprint
in your head, right?

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:29.090
How should the pages
that are made look like,

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:30.760
because then you don't need to focus

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:32.410
so much on the design anymore,

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:34.450
because you already know
how you will arrange

00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:36.537
the information, namely
in the form of a table.

00:18:36.537 --> 00:18:39.010
And then you can kind of the
limited time that you have,

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:42.801
spent much more effectively
by just kind of focusing

00:18:42.801 --> 00:18:46.540
on the content, and then
the design just follows

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:48.470
this table format that you can just use

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:50.410
as a blueprint for all your pages.

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:53.240
Building on that I would like
to show you a slightly more

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:56.070
kind of complicated, a
bit more advanced page

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:59.720
that is still created following
the exact same principle.

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:01.473
So this is a page and just to guide you

00:19:01.473 --> 00:19:02.890
through the logic, right,

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:06.451
this is a page showing
the industry market volume

00:19:06.451 --> 00:19:08.610
of different industries and then

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:10.630
kind of identify opportunities

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:13.030
that this company isn't currently serving.

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:16.630
So it looks at the total
industry, these 922 million,

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:18.670
it looks at what part
of it is already served

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:20.110
by the company we're looking at.

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:22.690
It looks at kind of a
segment of the industry,

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:24.430
which we cannot serve at the moment

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:26.980
because we lacked
relevant product offering.

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:29.320
And then we identify an opportunity

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:30.800
that we currently do not serve,

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:33.253
but actually have the relevant offering.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:37.200
So this is probably a
bit more advanced logic.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:39.480
And then what you see here,
as well as that there's

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:41.960
something like a double
click on this field, right.

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:44.510
So this nicely fits into
the double click principle

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:46.560
that we talked about earlier.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:48.270
So what is happening here is we basically

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:49.932
make a virtual double click

00:19:49.932 --> 00:19:52.340
on this potential opportunities row.

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:54.363
And then it expands here on the right,

00:19:54.363 --> 00:19:57.020
that we detail the five industries

00:19:57.020 --> 00:19:59.197
that we see as potential
opportunities here.

00:19:59.197 --> 00:20:02.998
And then provide bit more
information about what we think,

00:20:02.998 --> 00:20:04.360
excuse me,

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.080
what we think are the next steps here

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:07.483
that we need to look into.

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:09.900
But coming back to the principle,

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:13.087
every page has a table, in
this rather complex design,

00:20:13.087 --> 00:20:15.980
you still see that it's
basically just a page

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.930
which is exactly two columns,

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:19.590
first industry analysis,

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:21.290
and then this detailed column.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:23.560
And then we have these
individual rows here,

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:25.243
which represent parts of the industry

00:20:25.243 --> 00:20:27.172
that we segment in this way.

00:20:27.172 --> 00:20:29.930
And now by looking at this bubble,

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:31.520
which says by the legend here

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:33.200
on the top number of industries,

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:36.040
so this represents the number
of different industries

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:37.911
that we see into these categories.

00:20:37.911 --> 00:20:40.120
And I mean, this again,

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:42.210
can be seen this another column

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:43.330
of the table as well, right?

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:45.060
It doesn't have a header in the sense here

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:47.860
that something's stated here.

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:51.170
But the way it is put here is the column.

00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:52.870
And let's put beside of course,

00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:56.140
or within the role that represents
the respective category,

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.693
where we see this number
of industries to fit.

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:01.936
So again, the principle,

00:21:01.936 --> 00:21:03.710
every purchaser table,
and you can see that

00:21:03.710 --> 00:21:07.097
you can use this kind
of framework to expand

00:21:07.097 --> 00:21:09.560
on one of the row elements that you have,

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:12.050
if you wanna provide some details.

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:14.770
And this is often helpful if
you want to kind of provide

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:17.680
details on the most important role.

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:19.590
And this probably would be a good example

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:23.040
because the role of the
potential opportunities is indeed

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:24.690
the most important one of this page,

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:26.410
it is the row

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:28.850
that you wouldn't really
want to provide the dates on.

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:30.740
But you can also use it if you

00:21:30.740 --> 00:21:32.210
kind of in a bit more arbitrary

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:34.960
or selective way you have a
list of different elements

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:36.092
that you talk about.

00:21:36.092 --> 00:21:38.470
And then maybe you just
wanna go into detail,

00:21:38.470 --> 00:21:39.740
into one of these elements.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:41.250
Maybe also because you have the most

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:42.470
interesting things to show

00:21:42.470 --> 00:21:44.140
for this specific element.

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:45.640
Then in a bit arbitrary way,

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:47.167
you can make a callout just like that

00:21:47.167 --> 00:21:48.800
and double click just like that,

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:51.644
where you selectively provide
details on the specific

00:21:51.644 --> 00:21:53.943
element of your table.

00:21:56.599 --> 00:21:58.973
And kind of making this even more general,

00:21:58.973 --> 00:22:02.923
you can use a blueprint just
as depicted on this page,

00:22:02.923 --> 00:22:06.370
as just the general
blueprint that is applicable

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:09.500
whenever you have any
kind of data analysis,

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:11.470
that you would like to visualize.

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:14.140
And kind of this very
simple basic blueprint

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:16.720
that you can use for many
different types of pages

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:17.700
goes like this,

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:19.220
that in the middle of the page,

